 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Shortest dominating-set TAR reconfiguration generator
     2	
     3	Status: the native generator and all local gates pass, but the mandatory four-vendor oracle run is **infrastructure-blocked**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` for every call, so this directory does not yet contain a valid hardened/too-easy verdict and must not be submitted as hardened.
     4	
     5	| Profile field | Value |
     6	|---|---|
     7	| Track | **B** — an efficient mechanical algorithm is disclosed below |
     8	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     9	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    10	| Computational core | graph |
    11	| Certificate form | exact symbolic word of `A<vertex>` / `R<vertex>` TAR moves |
    12	| Intuition | constraint propagation through a uniquely peelable alternating incidence order |
    13	| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    14	
    15	## Problem and trust model
    16	
    17	This family uses the paper's native object: a finite graph, two dominating sets, a token limit, and the token-addition/removal (TAR) rule defined in Section 1 of Bousquet, Joffard, and Ouvrard, [*Linear transformations between dominating sets in the TAR-model*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16726). The solver must give a shortest word of typed vertex moves (`A12` adds vertex 12; `R7` removes vertex 7). The verifier replays every move, checks membership, the size bound, exact closed-neighborhood domination after each removal, and the target endpoint. It accepts any valid sequence and never reads the planted answer.
    18	
    19	Generation is inverse: a random total precedence order is sampled first, ordinary degree-two graph vertices encode pair and precedence constraints, the legal sequence is recorded, and only then are all graph vertices randomly relabelled. A private pair constraint forces each target addition to be followed by its matching source removal. Consecutive precedence constraints force one order; redundant forward constraints and identically shaped decoy pairs obscure it. Thus there is exactly one certificate in the declared `(n!)^2` endpoint-respecting language, known without solving the generated instance.
    20	
    21	## Why Track B
    22	
    23	The paper itself rules out an unqualified Track A story. Section 1 cites PSPACE-completeness for general TAR reachability but also linear-time algorithms on trees, interval graphs, and cographs, plus an FPT algorithm parameterized by `k` on fixed-`K_{d,d}`-free graphs. Section 3, Theorem 2 constructively gives a polynomial-time linear path at `k = Gamma(G) + alpha(G) - 1`; Section 4, Lemma 4 treats minor-sparse graphs, and Section 5, Theorem 6 treats bounded-treewidth graphs constructively at their stated high thresholds.
    24	
    25	For this generated subclass, the disclosed reference algorithm scans missing target vertices and remaining source vertices until exact domination identifies the next legal pair. Its complexity is `O(n^3 (|V|+|E|))`; at configured shipping `n=32`, the final audited run over eight seeds averaged **roughly 0.05 s, 1,041,824 exact membership tests, and 5,583 candidate pairs**, solving 8/8 as expected. The compact route recognizes that degree-two constraints between endpoint-difference vertices have a unique leaf-peeling order, then emits the corresponding add/remove pair. That route costs at most **143 link/move operations**. The benchmark tests noticing this compression, not computational intractability.
    26	
    27	## Worked demo
    28	
    29	The `demo` preset with seed 3 is hand-solvable. It renders:
    30	
    31	```text
    32	Vertices: 1..19; k=7
    33	Source: 2 6 7 12 15 18
    34	Target: 6 7 10 11 12 14
    35	Edges:
    36	8-13 7-13 7-8 7-15 2-5 14-16 7-18 5-14 6-19
    37	4-11 16-18 4-18 2-7 7-12 7-11 9-10 6-7 1-13
    38	7-17 3-10 2-3 8-19 1-12 9-15 7-10 7-14
    39	Required output: exactly 6 typed moves, `A`=add and `R`=remove.
    40	```
    41	
    42	The planted answer is `<answer>["A10","R15","A14","R2","A11","R18"]</answer>`, and `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Swapping the first two moves gives `["R15","A10","A14","R2","A11","R18"]`, rejected as `(False, "step 1 does not dominate vertex 9")`. A person can solve this demo by tracing the three pair constraints; the larger presets preserve the idea while random labels, redundant constraints, and decoys make manual tracing costly.
    43	
    44	## Difficulty presets
    45	
    46	| Preset | Core swaps `n` | Decoy pairs | Extra precedence | Noise edges | Vertices | Answer atoms | Status |
    47	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    48	| demo | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 19 | 6 | hand example |
    49	| easy | 32 | 48 | 16 | 36 | 289 | 64 | configured shipping preset; oracle pending |
    50	| medium | 44 | 66 | 22 | 66 | 397 | 88 | not reached |
    51	| hard | 60 | 90 | 30 | 100 | 541 | 120 | not reached |
    52	
    53	`escalate()` first grows decoy and redundant-constraint haystacks at fixed answer length. It returns `cap_bound` before either the 256-atom or 300-operation limit would be crossed.
    54	
    55	## Local gate results
    56	
    57	| Gate | Result |
    58	|---|---|
    59	| G1 | 12/12 planted paths verified; all answers JSON-native |
    60	| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
    61	| G3 | tagged JSON round-trip passed; garbage returns `None` |
    62	| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; declared space has 236 bits at shipping |
    63	| G5 | demo exact solution count 1; shipping sampled density 0/200,000; reference cost 1,041,824 membership tests |
    64	| G6 | degree, smallest-label greedy, 256 restarts, and one-shot private-neighbor attacks: 0/8 successes each; reference and compact solvers: 8/8 |
    65	| G7 | doubled `n=64` instance built and verified; candidate space grew from 236 to 592 bits |
    66	| G8 | 140/140 relabelling/composition invariance checks and 140/140 carried witnesses passed; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    67	| G9(c) | 421 actual / 449 worst-case characters, 64 atoms, 143 intended operations: within every cap |
    68	
    69	## Oracle loop and G9 arms
    70	
    71	The bare script run made no countable attempt: all four retries failed before a reply. These rows are retained in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` rather than rewritten or scored as model failures.
    72	
    73	| Arm | Preset | Seeds | Result | Why |
    74	|---|---|---|---|---|
    75	| bare | easy | 2144650319, 48214867, 1091896101, 1490615685 | 0 solved / 0 attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
    76	| structural | easy | 881993767, 1870000606, 464593307, 970153363 | 0 solved / 0 attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
    77	| placebo | easy | 780100353, 1090011423, 1714892654, 333658303 | 0 solved / 0 attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
    78	
    79	`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined as an oracle comparison (stored numerically as 0.0 only because both have zero countable attempts). No conclusion about hint usefulness is justified. The structural hint names only the alternating-path invariant; it does not give the peeling procedure. Re-run all three arms after replenishing the OpenRouter key.
    80	
    81	## Use
    82	
    83	```python
    84	import gen_2006_16726 as g
    85	
    86	inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
    87	prompt = g.render(inst)
    88	candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
    89	assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
    90	```
    91	
    92	From the repository root, emission is `bash scripts/emit.sh 2006.16726 20`. Do not submit yet: first rerun `python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2006_16726.py` here, run the structural and placebo copies in isolated directories, update `G9_ORACLE_RESULTS`, regenerate `selftest_report.json`, and require a script-owned `hardened` verdict.
    93	
    94	## Caveats
    95	
    96	The sampled `P(guess)` is for the deliberately strong prior that already knows the exact length, endpoint symmetric difference, alternation, and token-capacity pattern; it does not estimate arbitrary language-model behavior. The distribution is Track B and is easy for the disclosed polynomial legal-pair scan. Static attacks were tested, but no SAT/SMT encoding, bidirectional reconfiguration BFS, or learned graph heuristic was run; general BFS would also be a valid but much more expensive reference. The canonical key uses colored 1-dimensional Weisfeiler-Leman refinement, which passed all generated relabellings but is not a complete graph-isomorphism canonizer. Most importantly, the mandatory multi-vendor evidence is absent because of the external key limit, so local gates alone must not be read as a completed hardness claim.

